% 49-differential-testing.weft — Differential testing against clang.
%
% Each program is compiled by BOTH kcc and clang, linked freestanding,
% run, and their exit codes compared.  clang is the oracle: any
% disagreement on a well-defined program is a kcc code-generation bug.

\chapter{Differential Testing}
\label{ch:differential-testing}

The code-generation suite of Chapter~\ref{ch:codegen-tests} checks each
program against an exit code computed by hand.  That is a hard ceiling:
a test can only be as complex as a value one is willing to work out on
paper.  Differential testing lifts the ceiling.  It compiles each
program with \kcc{} \emph{and} with \texttt{clang}, runs both, and
compares---using \texttt{clang} as an oracle for what the program
\emph{should} do.  No expected value is written down; the reference
compiler supplies it.  This is how the self-hosting tokenizer of
Chapter~\ref{ch:self-hosting-lexer} was validated, generalised here into
a standing suite.

The programs are freestanding: each is an \texttt{int main(void)} whose
return value becomes the process exit code, linked with entry
\texttt{\_main} and no C runtime---the same shape \kcc{}'s own tests
use, and one \texttt{clang} honours too.  Because an exit code is a
single byte, programs return small results (or reduce modulo a small
number); a genuine miscompilation practically never lands on an exact
multiple of~256 away from the correct answer, so agreement on the byte
is agreement in fact.

Each program lands in one of three buckets.  \textbf{ok}: both compilers
agree.  \textbf{mismatch}: both compile and run but disagree---a
code-generation defect, the bug this suite exists to catch.
\textbf{gap}: \texttt{clang} compiles it but \kcc{} cannot---a missing
language feature, reported but not counted as a failure, since it maps
the frontier of what \kcc{} accepts rather than a wrong answer.

\section{The Runner}

The runner compiles with the oracle first: if \texttt{clang} rejects a
program the program itself is wrong, and it is skipped.  Otherwise it
records \texttt{clang}'s exit code, then tries \kcc{}.  A \kcc{}
compile failure is a gap; a successful compile is linked, run, and its
exit code compared against the oracle's.

@d Difftest: runner @{
#!/bin/bash
# kcc differential test suite (vs clang) — generated by weft, do not edit
set -u

PASS=0
FAIL=0
GAP=0
XFAIL=0
TOTAL=0
KCC="tmp/kcc"
TMP="/tmp/kcc_difftest_$$"
SDK=$(xcrun --show-sdk-path 2>/dev/null)

mkdir -p "$TMP"
trap 'rm -rf "$TMP"' EXIT

# Link an object freestanding and run it; echo the exit code, or "L" if the
# link fails (kcc emitted an object the system linker rejects).
link_run() {
    if ld "$1" -o "$2" -lSystem -syslibroot "$SDK" -e _main -arch arm64 2>/dev/null; then
        "$2" >/dev/null 2>&1
        echo $?
    else
        echo "L"
    fi
}

difftest() {
    local name="$1"
    local src="$2"
    TOTAL=$((TOTAL + 1))
    printf '%s\n' "$src" > "$TMP/d.c"

    # Oracle: clang must accept the program, else it is not valid C.
    if ! clang -c "$TMP/d.c" -o "$TMP/dc.o" 2>/dev/null; then
        printf "  %-28s SKIP (clang rejects)\n" "$name"
        return
    fi
    local crc
    crc=$(link_run "$TMP/dc.o" "$TMP/dc")

    # kcc: a compile failure is a feature gap, not a wrong answer.
    if ! $KCC -c "$TMP/d.c" -o "$TMP/dk.o" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        GAP=$((GAP + 1))
        printf "  %-28s GAP (kcc cannot compile)\n" "$name"
        return
    fi
    local krc
    krc=$(link_run "$TMP/dk.o" "$TMP/dk")

    if [ "$krc" = "$crc" ]; then
        PASS=$((PASS + 1))
        printf "  %-28s ok (%s)\n" "$name" "$krc"
    else
        FAIL=$((FAIL + 1))
        printf "  %-28s MISMATCH kcc=%s clang=%s\n" "$name" "$krc" "$crc"
    fi
}

# Like difftest, but the divergence is a documented, expected limitation.
# It is reported but never fails the build.  If it unexpectedly agrees, the
# limitation has been resolved and the case should graduate to difftest.
difftest_xfail() {
    local name="$1"
    local src="$2"
    local reason="$3"
    TOTAL=$((TOTAL + 1))
    printf '%s\n' "$src" > "$TMP/d.c"
    if ! clang -c "$TMP/d.c" -o "$TMP/dc.o" 2>/dev/null; then
        printf "  %-28s SKIP (clang rejects)\n" "$name"
        return
    fi
    local crc
    crc=$(link_run "$TMP/dc.o" "$TMP/dc")
    if ! $KCC -c "$TMP/d.c" -o "$TMP/dk.o" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        GAP=$((GAP + 1))
        printf "  %-28s GAP (kcc cannot compile)\n" "$name"
        return
    fi
    local krc
    krc=$(link_run "$TMP/dk.o" "$TMP/dk")
    if [ "$krc" = "$crc" ]; then
        printf "  %-28s XPASS (divergence resolved: %s)\n" "$name" "$reason"
    else
        XFAIL=$((XFAIL + 1))
        printf "  %-28s xfail: %s (kcc=%s clang=%s)\n" "$name" "$reason" "$krc" "$crc"
    fi
}

echo "kcc differential test suite (vs clang)"
echo "======================================"
@}

\section{Arithmetic and Bit Manipulation}

Integer arithmetic is where an all-spill backend's shuttling of values
through scratch registers is most likely to drop or corrupt a value.
These programs stress operator precedence, signed and unsigned division
and remainder (well-defined truncation toward zero in C), shifts, and
the bitwise operators---keeping every result in defined territory.

@d Difftest: arithmetic @{
echo ""
echo "--- Arithmetic and Bits ---"

difftest "precedence-1" \
    "int main(void){ return 2 + 3 * 4 - 6 / 2; }"
difftest "precedence-2" \
    "int main(void){ return (2 + 3) * (4 - 1) % 7; }"
difftest "signed-div-neg" \
    "int main(void){ int a = -17; int b = 5; return 100 + a / b; }"
difftest "signed-mod-neg" \
    "int main(void){ int a = -17; int b = 5; return 100 + a % b; }"
difftest "unsigned-div" \
    "int main(void){ unsigned int a = 4000000000; unsigned int b = 7; return (int)(a / b % 200); }"
difftest "shifts" \
    "int main(void){ int a = 1; return (a << 5) + (256 >> 3); }"
difftest "bitwise-mix" \
    "int main(void){ int a = 0xF0; int b = 0x3C; return (a & b) | (a ^ b); }"
difftest "chained-cmp" \
    "int main(void){ int a = 5; int b = 7; int c = 5; return (a < b) + (a == c) + (b > c); }"
difftest "unary-neg-not" \
    "int main(void){ int a = 5; return (-a + 10) + (~a & 7) + (!0); }"
@}

\section{Control Flow}

Branch lowering, loop back-edges, and \texttt{switch} dispatch are
exercised here: nested conditionals, each loop form, fall-through, and
\texttt{break}/\texttt{continue}.

@d Difftest: control @{
echo ""
echo "--- Control Flow ---"

difftest "nested-if" \
    "int main(void){ int x = 7; if (x > 5) { if (x < 10) return 1; return 2; } return 3; }"
difftest "while-sum" \
    "int main(void){ int s = 0; int i = 1; while (i <= 10) { s = s + i; i = i + 1; } return s; }"
difftest "do-while" \
    "int main(void){ int n = 0; int i = 0; do { n = n + 2; i = i + 1; } while (i < 6); return n; }"
difftest "for-product" \
    "int main(void){ int p = 1; int i = 1; for (i = 1; i <= 5; i = i + 1) p = p * i; return p % 200; }"
difftest "switch-fallthrough" \
    "int main(void){ int x = 2; int r = 0; switch (x) { case 1: r = r + 1; case 2: r = r + 10; case 3: r = r + 100; break; default: r = 255; } return r; }"
difftest "break-continue" \
    "int main(void){ int s = 0; int i = 0; while (i < 20) { i = i + 1; if (i == 5) continue; if (i == 12) break; s = s + i; } return s; }"
difftest "nested-loops" \
    "int main(void){ int c = 0; int i = 0; for (i = 0; i < 5; i = i + 1) { int j = 0; for (j = 0; j < 5; j = j + 1) c = c + 1; } return c; }"
@}

\section{Functions and Recursion}

Calling convention, argument passing, and return values are exercised
by recursion (single and mutual) and multi-argument calls.

@d Difftest: functions @{
echo ""
echo "--- Functions and Recursion ---"

difftest "factorial" \
    "int fact(int n){ if (n <= 1) return 1; return n * fact(n - 1); } int main(void){ return fact(5) % 200; }"
difftest "fib" \
    "int fib(int n){ if (n < 2) return n; return fib(n-1) + fib(n-2); } int main(void){ return fib(11); }"
difftest "mutual-rec" \
    "int is_even(int n); int is_odd(int n){ if (n == 0) return 0; return is_even(n - 1); } int is_even(int n){ if (n == 0) return 1; return is_odd(n - 1); } int main(void){ return is_even(10) + is_odd(7); }"
difftest "many-args" \
    "int sum6(int a, int b, int c, int d, int e, int f){ return a + b + c + d + e + f; } int main(void){ return sum6(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6); }"
difftest "accumulate" \
    "int step(int acc, int x){ return acc + x * x; } int main(void){ int acc = 0; int i = 1; while (i <= 6) { acc = step(acc, i); i = i + 1; } return acc % 250; }"
@}

\section{Pointers, Arrays, and Structs}

Aggregate access and pointer arithmetic---subscripting, member access
through pointers, address-of, and passing addresses to functions---%
are the patterns most sensitive to the address-computation paths.

@d Difftest: aggregates @{
echo ""
echo "--- Pointers, Arrays, Structs ---"

difftest "array-sum" \
    "int main(void){ int a[6]; int i = 0; for (i = 0; i < 6; i = i + 1) a[i] = i * i; int s = 0; for (i = 0; i < 6; i = i + 1) s = s + a[i]; return s; }"
difftest "array-init-list" \
    "int main(void){ int a[5] = {3, 1, 4, 1, 5}; return a[0] + a[2] + a[4]; }"
difftest "pointer-swap" \
    "void swap(int *a, int *b){ int t = *a; *a = *b; *b = t; } int main(void){ int x = 10; int y = 3; swap(&x, &y); return x - y; }"
difftest "struct-fields" \
    "struct P { int x; int y; }; int main(void){ struct P p; p.x = 12; p.y = 30; return p.x + p.y; }"
difftest "struct-ptr" \
    "struct P { int x; int y; }; int dist(struct P *p){ return p->x + p->y; } int main(void){ struct P p; p.x = 40; p.y = 2; return dist(&p); }"
difftest "struct-array" \
    "struct P { int a; int b; }; int main(void){ struct P v[3]; int i = 0; for (i = 0; i < 3; i = i + 1) { v[i].a = i; v[i].b = i * 10; } return v[2].a + v[2].b; }"
difftest "addr-of-elem" \
    "void set(int *p, int v){ *p = v; } int main(void){ int a[4]; int i = 0; for (i = 0; i < 4; i = i + 1) set(&a[i], i + 1); return a[0] + a[1] + a[2] + a[3]; }"
difftest "ptr-to-ptr" \
    "int main(void){ int x = 42; int *p = &x; int **pp = &p; **pp = 7; return x; }"
@}

\section{Operators and Edge Cases}

The remaining operators---ternary, comma, compound assignment,
increment/decrement, short-circuit logicals---and a few numeric edge
cases round out the corpus.

@d Difftest: operators @{
echo ""
echo "--- Operators and Edge Cases ---"

difftest "ternary-chain" \
    "int main(void){ int x = 3; return x == 1 ? 10 : x == 2 ? 20 : x == 3 ? 30 : 40; }"
difftest "comma" \
    "int main(void){ int a = 0; int b = (a = 5, a + 3); return a + b; }"
difftest "compound-assign" \
    "int main(void){ int a = 10; a += 5; a *= 2; a -= 3; a /= 4; return a; }"
difftest "inc-dec" \
    "int main(void){ int a = 5; int b = a++; int c = ++a; return a + b + c; }"
difftest "short-circuit" \
    "int side(int *n){ *n = *n + 1; return 1; } int main(void){ int n = 0; if (0 && side(&n)) n = n + 100; if (1 || side(&n)) n = n + 10; return n; }"
difftest "sizeof-mix" \
    "int main(void){ return sizeof(int) + sizeof(char) + sizeof(long); }"
difftest "char-arith" \
    "int main(void){ char c = 'A'; int d = c + 1; return d; }"
difftest "global-counter" \
    "int g = 100; void bump(void){ g = g + 7; } int main(void){ bump(); bump(); bump(); return g % 200; }"
difftest "static-local" \
    "int next(void){ static int c = 0; c = c + 1; return c; } int main(void){ next(); next(); return next() + next(); }"
@}

\section{Deeper Combinations}

A second batch reaches for feature \emph{combinations}: signed
comparisons and shifts of negatives, whole-\texttt{struct} assignment,
nested structs, arrays of pointers, multi-level indirection, register
pressure from many live locals, explicit narrowing casts, and a
function pointer stored in a \texttt{struct} field---the last a known
frontier that surfaces as a gap.

@d Difftest: combinations @{
echo ""
echo "--- Deeper Combinations ---"

difftest "neg-compare" \
    "int main(void){ int a = -5; int b = 3; return (a < b) * 100 + (a <= b) * 10 + (b > a); }"
difftest "ashr-negative" \
    "int main(void){ int a = -64; return (a >> 2) + 200; }"
difftest "mod-signs" \
    "int main(void){ return (17 % 5) + (-17 % 5) + (17 % -5) + 100; }"
difftest "nested-struct" \
    "struct I { int x; int y; }; struct O { struct I p; int z; }; int main(void){ struct O o; o.p.x = 1; o.p.y = 2; o.z = 3; return o.p.x + o.p.y + o.z; }"
difftest "struct-assign" \
    "struct P { int a; int b; int c; }; int main(void){ struct P x; x.a = 5; x.b = 6; x.c = 7; struct P y; y = x; return y.a + y.b + y.c; }"
difftest "array-of-ptr" \
    "int main(void){ int a = 1; int b = 2; int c = 3; int *p[3]; p[0] = &a; p[1] = &b; p[2] = &c; int s = 0; int i = 0; while (i < 3) { s = s + *p[i]; i = i + 1; } return s; }"
difftest "triple-deref" \
    "int main(void){ int x = 9; int *p = &x; int **q = &p; int ***r = &q; ***r = 42; return x; }"
difftest "deep-arith" \
    "int main(void){ int a = 2; int b = 3; int c = 4; int d = 5; return ((a + b) * (c + d) - (a * b)) / (c - a) % 50; }"
difftest "many-locals" \
    "int main(void){ int a=1; int b=2; int c=3; int d=4; int e=5; int f=6; int g=7; int h=8; int i=9; int j=10; return a+b+c+d+e+f+g+h+i+j; }"
difftest "string-index" \
    "int main(void){ char *s = \"hello\"; return s[0] + s[4]; }"
difftest "cast-narrow" \
    "int main(void){ int a = 300; char c = (char)a; return (int)c + 100; }"
difftest "fnptr-in-struct" \
    "struct Ops { int (*f)(int, int); }; int add(int a, int b){ return a + b; } int main(void){ struct Ops o; o.f = add; return o.f(20, 22); }"
@}

\section{Known Divergences}
\label{sec:difftest-divergences}

The first bug this suite caught was not a bug at all but a documented
limitation of the backend.  \kcc{}'s all-spill model keeps every value
in a 64-bit slot and computes with 64-bit instructions; it does
\emph{not} truncate an \texttt{int} or \texttt{unsigned int} result back
to 32~bits.  For values that never approach $2^{32}$ this is invisible,
and the whole corpus above agrees with \texttt{clang}.  But an unsigned
computation that wraps past $2^{32}$---\texttt{0u - 1}, or a sum that
overflows 32~bits---keeps \kcc{}'s extra high bits, so the two compilers
part ways.  Modelling declared integer widths (masking or
sign-extending results to the type) is a Stage~9a task; until then these
cases are recorded as \emph{expected} divergences, reported but not
failing the build.  Should \kcc{} ever agree, the runner flags an
\texttt{XPASS} so the entry can graduate to a real test.

@d Difftest: divergences @{
echo ""
echo "--- Known Divergences (32-bit width, Stage 9a) ---"

difftest_xfail "u32-wrap-sub" \
    "int main(void){ unsigned int a = 0; a = a - 1; return (int)(a % 251); }" \
    "no 32-bit truncation"
difftest_xfail "u32-wrap-add" \
    "int main(void){ unsigned int a = 0xFFFFFFFF; a = a + 1; return (int)(a % 100); }" \
    "no 32-bit truncation"
difftest_xfail "u32-overflow-sum" \
    "int main(void){ unsigned int a = 4000000000; unsigned int b = 1000000000; return (int)((a + b) % 1000); }" \
    "no 32-bit truncation"
@}

\section{Results}

The suite fails only on a mismatch---a genuine wrong answer.  Gaps
(features \kcc{} cannot yet compile) and expected divergences
(\S\ref{sec:difftest-divergences}) are tallied and reported so both
frontiers stay visible, but neither fails the build.

@d Difftest: results @{
echo ""
echo "======================================"
printf "diff results: %d ok, %d mismatch, %d gap, %d xfail (of %d)\n" \
    "$PASS" "$FAIL" "$GAP" "$XFAIL" "$TOTAL"
if [ "$FAIL" -ne 0 ]; then
    exit 1
fi
@}
